1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:11,075
Here's one gaming PC running a demanding game at 48 frames per second, and by the end of this video it'll be in the mid 80s without buying a single part.

2
00:00:11,354 --> 00:00:21,093
Along the way we'll test five popular tricks that people swear by and see why they barely move the number, so you know exactly where your time is worth spending.

3
00:00:21,843 --> 00:00:24,430
First, what is that number actually measuring?

4
00:00:24,710 --> 00:00:26,608
Every frame is a relay race.

5
00:00:26,888 --> 00:00:34,675
The CPU runs the game logic and tells the graphics card what to draw, then the GPU paints the pixels.

6
00:00:34,955 --> 00:00:39,910
At 48 frames per second, each frame takes about 21 milliseconds.

7
00:00:40,190 --> 00:00:44,584
To reach 60, you need to get that down to about 16.7.

8
00:00:44,864 --> 00:00:52,133
Whichever runner is slower sets the pace, and that single idea explains every fix and every myth that follows.

9
00:00:52,883 --> 00:01:00,683
Our example PC is GPU-limited: the graphics card is at 99 percent, while the CPU waits for it.

10
00:01:00,963 --> 00:01:03,708
So the first fix is the cheapest one there is.

11
00:01:03,988 --> 00:01:06,162
Drop the preset from Ultra to High.

12
00:01:06,442 --> 00:01:15,130
TechSpot tested this across a whole set of games and found the jump from Ultra to High is typically worth 15 to 25 percent.

13
00:01:15,410 --> 00:01:19,557
In Cyberpunk 2077 it was 24 percent.

14
00:01:19,837 --> 00:01:25,823
In Microsoft Flight Simulator at 4K, the average went from 45 to 57.

15
00:01:26,103 --> 00:01:30,628
Ultra mostly adds effects that are hard to spot while you're actually playing.

16
00:01:31,378 --> 00:01:35,282
Applied to our example, 48 becomes about 58.

17
00:01:35,562 --> 00:01:40,105
Notice that the frame time bar shrank, but the picture barely changed.

18
00:01:40,385 --> 00:01:46,062
That's the trade you want: big savings in milliseconds for a small cost you can't see in motion.

19
00:01:46,342 --> 00:01:48,200
And you don't have to drop everything.

20
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:58,774
Pick the preset first, then put textures back up if your graphics card has the memory for them, because texture quality costs memory far more than it costs speed.

21
00:01:59,524 --> 00:02:01,316
The second fix is upscaling.

22
00:02:01,596 --> 00:02:11,136
With DLSS, FSR or XeSS, the GPU draws a smaller image and a smart upscaler rebuilds it at full resolution.

23
00:02:11,416 --> 00:02:17,289
In DLSS Quality mode, each side of the image is rendered at about 67 percent.

24
00:02:17,569 --> 00:02:24,943
At 1440p, that's roughly 1707 by 960.

25
00:02:25,223 --> 00:02:31,714
Because that shrinks both width and height, the GPU paints only about 44 percent of the pixels.

26
00:02:31,994 --> 00:02:37,077
Performance mode goes to 50 percent per side, which is a quarter of the pixels.

27
00:02:37,357 --> 00:02:41,497
In our example, Quality mode lifts 58 to about 78.

28
00:02:42,247 --> 00:02:46,687
The third fix lives in the BIOS, and lots of people never turn it on.

29
00:02:46,967 --> 00:02:53,131
Memory runs at a slow default speed until you enable its XMP or EXPO profile.

30
00:02:53,411 --> 00:03:05,433
PC Gamer tested the same DDR5 kit at its rated 6000 and at the default 4800 on a Ryzen 9 9900X.

31
00:03:05,713 --> 00:03:17,433
In Hogwarts Legacy at 1080p, the average went from 154 to 183, and the 1% lows went from 65 to 78.

32
00:03:17,713 --> 00:03:21,049
At 4K, the averages were basically identical.

33
00:03:21,329 --> 00:03:28,357
Memory speed helps the CPU's side of the relay, so it matters most when the CPU is the slower runner.

34
00:03:28,637 --> 00:03:34,075
For our example, it smooths the dips and adds a few frames, landing in the mid 80s.

35
00:03:34,825 --> 00:03:39,149
Now the myths, starting with the most common one: buy more RAM.

36
00:03:39,429 --> 00:03:48,031
PC Gamer also ran 16 gigabytes against 32 gigabytes on the same machine with an RTX 5090.

37
00:03:48,311 --> 00:03:50,830
Cyberpunk went from 83 to 84.

38
00:03:51,110 --> 00:03:54,097
Black Myth Wukong was 76 either way.

39
00:03:54,377 --> 00:04:04,771
Some games did gain: Escape from Tarkov's average rose from 97 to 109, and Flight Simulator stuttered less on takeoff and landing.

40
00:04:05,051 --> 00:04:10,482
But if your game fits in 16 gigabytes, the extra memory just sits there unused.

41
00:04:10,762 --> 00:04:14,182
More RAM fixes running out, not running slow.

42
00:04:14,932 --> 00:04:17,968
Myth two: a faster drive raises your frame rate.

43
00:04:18,248 --> 00:04:21,572
Storage feeds the game its files, not its frames.

44
00:04:21,852 --> 00:04:35,599
In a Digital Citizen test of an SSD against a hard drive, Far Cry 5 ran 1 frame per second faster on the SSD, and their conclusion was that an SSD doesn't increase your frame rate.

45
00:04:35,879 --> 00:04:39,493
What it does change is how long you wait on loading screens.

46
00:04:39,773 --> 00:04:43,167
That's a real upgrade, just not an FPS upgrade.

47
00:04:43,917 --> 00:04:47,792
Myth three: frame generation doubles your real performance.

48
00:04:48,072 --> 00:04:53,481
Frame generation inserts an extra, predicted frame between two real ones.

49
00:04:53,761 --> 00:05:05,431
AMD says it roughly doubles the frame rate, but it recommends at least 60 real frames per second before you turn it on, and says that below 30 it should be avoided entirely.

50
00:05:05,711 --> 00:05:10,118
The reason is simple: the inserted frames don't respond to your mouse.

51
00:05:10,398 --> 00:05:19,205
Your controls still react at the real rate, which is why NVIDIA requires its Reflex latency tool whenever its version is on.

52
00:05:19,485 --> 00:05:21,120
It's smoothness, not speed.

53
00:05:21,870 --> 00:05:26,755
Myth four is the booster app that promises free frames by clearing your memory.

54
00:05:27,035 --> 00:05:34,890
Think back to the RAM test: doubling the memory barely changed the frame rate, so freeing a few gigabytes won't either.

55
00:05:35,170 --> 00:05:38,875
Myth five is that lowering your resolution always helps.

56
00:05:39,155 --> 00:05:42,256
It only helps when the GPU is the slower runner.

57
00:05:42,536 --> 00:05:53,670
If the CPU needs 12 milliseconds to prepare each frame, you're capped at around 83 frames per second, no matter how few pixels the GPU has to draw.

58
00:05:54,420 --> 00:05:57,467
So here's our example PC, back at the start.

59
00:05:57,747 --> 00:06:00,305
Ultra to High took 48 to 58.

60
00:06:00,585 --> 00:06:02,476
Upscaling took it to 78.

61
00:06:02,756 --> 00:06:05,728
The memory profile pushed it into the mid 80s.

62
00:06:06,008 --> 00:06:10,938
None of it cost money, and all of it works because it shortened the slower runner's lap.

63
00:06:11,218 --> 00:06:20,981
Turn on frame generation now and the counter might read around 160, which looks great, but it'll still feel like the 80s underneath.

64
00:06:21,731 --> 00:06:27,629
Here's the check you can run tonight: open a performance overlay and look at GPU usage.

65
00:06:27,909 --> 00:06:32,342
If it sits near 99 percent, lower settings and use upscaling.

66
00:06:32,622 --> 00:06:42,135
If it's well below that, your CPU or memory is the limit, so turn on XMP or EXPO, and lower resolution won't save you.

67
00:06:42,415 --> 00:06:49,755
Change one thing at a time, and watch the frame time, not just the counter, so you know which change actually helped.

68
00:06:50,505 --> 00:06:59,630
That second case has a name, a bottleneck, and next time we'll find out exactly how to tell whether your CPU is holding back your GPU.

69
00:06:59,910 --> 00:07:09,528
If this video got you some free frames, subscribe so you don't miss it, and tell us in the comments what frame rate you're getting in your favorite game right now.

